----------
Browser ke paas actual session data nahi hota.
Browser ke paas sirf Session ID hoti hai.


## EXAMPLE 1: REGULAR CART:
----------------------------

Suppose tumne kisi E-commerce application me login kiya, jo HttpSession use karti hai.

Application pehle user ko authenticate karti hai
(i.e. username/password ya other credentials verify karti hai).
Authentication ke liye required user information (e.g. User ID, Roles, Permissions)
generally Database ya kisi Identity Provider se retrieve ki jaati hai.

Authentication successful hone ke baad application,
Servlet Container (e.g. Tomcat) se HttpSession create karwati hai.

Tomcat HttpSession create karta hai, usse server ki local memory (RAM) me store karta hai aur us HttpSession ko ek unique Session ID assign karta hai.
Uske baad application user ki required session-related information (e.g. User ID, Roles, Permissions) HttpSession me store kar deti hai.

Uske baad Tomcat response ke saath browser ko ek cookie bhejta hai jisme Session ID (e.g. JSESSIONID=ABC123XYZ) hoti hai.

Browser is cookie ko store kar leta hai.

Subsequent requests me agar cookie current request ke liye applicable ho,
to browser automatically JSESSIONID request ke saath bhej deta hai.

Ab jab user Orders, Profile ya Account page open karta hai,
to browser backend ko multiple API requests bhejta hai.

Application har request par user ke credentials ko dobara
Database ya kisi Identity Provider ke against verify nahi karti.

Iske bajay Tomcat Session ID ki help se corresponding
HttpSession retrieve karta hai.

Application us HttpSession se user ki information aur Roles
obtain karti hai aur unke basis par Authorization perform karti hai.

Agar user ke paas required permission hoti hai,
to request successfully process kar di jaati hai.


## EXAMPLE 2: ANONYMOUS CART
-----------------------------

Suppose tum Flipkart par bina login kiye products
cart me add karte ho.

Is time application ko user ki identity pata nahi hoti,
kyunki user abhi authenticate nahi hua hai.

Isliye application temporary shopping cart ko
HttpSession me store kar sakti hai.

Baad me jab user login karta hai,
to application us temporary cart ko user ke account se
associate karke Database me persist (store) kar deti hai.

Iske baad future requests me user apna cart
alag-alag devices ya browser sessions se bhi access
kar sakta hai.

Ye anonymous shopping cart implement karne ka
ek common approach hai.



## DOES HTTP SESSION EXPIRE? YA USER LIFETIME LOGIN REHTA HAI?
----------------------------------------------------------------

Haan.

HttpSession ka expiry time hota hai.

Generally session following situations me end ho sakti hai:

1. SESSION TIMEOUT (MOST COMMON)
---------------------------------

Agar user configured timeout period (e.g. 30 minutes) tak koi request nahi bhejta,

to Tomcat automatically HttpSession destroy kar deta hai.

Spring Boot Configuration:

    server.servlet.session.timeout=30m


2. USER LOGOUT
---------------

Jab user logout karta hai,

application

    session.invalidate();

call karke current HttpSession ko immediately
destroy kar deti hai.


3. SERVER RESTART
------------------

By default HttpSession ,server ki local memory (RAM)
me store hota hai.

Agar server restart ho jaye,

to RAM clear ho jaati hai,

aur saari HttpSessions bhi destroy ho jaata hain.


4. SERVER CRASH
----------------

Agar server crash ho jaye,

to RAM me stored saari HttpSessions
lost ho jaati hain.

Result:

User ko dobara login karna padta hai.


