## APPROACH 3 : EXTERNAL SESSION STORE (REDIS)
-------------------------------------------------------

Session Replication Horizontal Scaling ki session-related
problems ko solve kar deti hai.

Lekin jaise-jaise application horizontally scale hoti hai,
Session Replication ki apni limitations bhi saamne aati hain:

    - Memory (RAM) Overhead increase hota hai.
    - Network Traffic increase hota hai.
    - Large clusters me scalability reduce ho sakti hai.
    - Replication delay ki wajah se temporary consistency issues
      aa sakte hain.


## QUESTION :
--------------

Kya koi aisa approach ho sakta hai jisme HttpSession ko
har application server ki local memory me store aur
multiple servers ke saath replicate karne ki zarurat na pade?

Answer:

Yes.

Isi purpose ke liye

    External Session Store

use kiya jata hai.


## BASIC IDEA :
----------------

HttpSession ko har application server ki local memory (RAM)
me store karne ke bajay, ek external shared storage me store
kar diya jata hai.

Sabhi application servers isi shared session store se
HttpSession ko read aur update karte hain.


                    Load Balancer
                   /      |      \
                  v       v       v

            Server-1  Server-2  Server-3
                 \        |        /
                  \       |       /
                   \      |      /
                      Redis


Isse sabhi application servers ke across
HttpSessions ko replicate/synchronize karne ki
zarurat nahi hoti.

Isliye Session Replication ke:

    - Memory Overhead
    - Replication Overhead
    - Replication Lag

ko avoid kiya ja sakta hai.


## WHICH TECHNOLOGY CAN BE USED?
----------------------------------

External Session Store ke liye multiple technologies
use ki ja sakti hain:

    - Redis
    - Database
    - Hazelcast
    - Apache Ignite

Inme se distributed session management ke liye
Redis ek very common choice hai.


## WHY REDIS?
-------------

Redis ek in-memory (RAM) data store hai jo
high-speed read/write operations provide karta hai.

Isliye frequently accessed session data ke liye
Redis suitable hota hai.

Spring Boot applications me Redis ko
External Session Store ke roop me use karke
HttpSession ko application servers ki local memory
ke bajay Redis me maintain kiya ja sakta hai.


## HOW DOES IT WORK?
----------------------

Suppose application horizontally scaled hai aur
multiple servers par deployed hai.

### STEP-1 : USER LOGIN
------------------------

User login karta hai.

Load Balancer login request ko

    Server-1

par forward karta hai.


### STEP-2 : AUTHENTICATION & SESSION CREATION
-----------------------------------------------

Server-1 application (Spring Security) user ke
credentials authenticate karti hai.

Authentication successful hone ke baad
application Servlet Container (Tomcat) ke through
HttpSession create karti hai.


### STEP-3 : HTTP SESSION DATA IS STORED IN REDIS
---------------------------------------------

Traditional setup:

    HttpSession
         ↓
    Server-1 Local RAM


External Session Store:

    HttpSession
         ↓
       Redis


Session me required information store ki ja sakti hai,
for example:

    - User ID
    - Roles
    - Authentication Context
    - Session Metadata


### STEP-4 : JSESSIONID IS SENT TO BROWSER
-------------------------------------------

HttpSession create hone ke baad ek unique Session ID generate hoti hai aur woh Session ID us HttpSession ko assign kar di jaati hai.

Example:

    JSESSIONID = ABC123XYZ

Server HTTP response ke Set-Cookie header ke through Session ID browser ko bhejta hai.

Browser ke paas actual HttpSession data nahi hota sirf Session ID hoti hai.


### STEP-5 : NEXT REQUEST
--------------------------

Ab user ki next HTTP request aati hai.

Browser automatically JSESSIONID cookie ko HTTP request ke saath bhej deta hai.

Suppose Load Balancer is baar request ko

    Server-2

par forward karta hai.


### STEP-6 : SERVER-2 RETRIEVES SESSION FROM REDIS
---------------------------------------------------

Server-2 ke paas local memory me
ABC123XYZ wali HttpSession available nahi hai.

Lekin iski zarurat bhi nahi hai.

Server-2 JSESSIONID ki help se Redis se
corresponding session data retrieve kar leta hai.

Flow:

    JSESSIONID
         ↓
      Server-2
         ↓
       Redis
         ↓
    HTTP Session Data
         ↓
    User Identified
         ↓
    Request Processed


### STEP-7 : ANY SERVER CAN PROCESS THE REQUEST
-----------------------------------------------

Ab user ki request:

    Server-1
        OR
    Server-2
        OR
    Server-3

kisi bhi application server par ja sakti hai.

Har server same Redis Session Store se
corresponding HttpSession retrieve kar sakta hai.

Isliye:

    ❌ Sticky Sessions ki zarurat nahi
    ❌ Session Replication ki zarurat nahi

Aur application horizontally scale hone ke baad bhi
session shared state maintain kar sakti hai.


## NOTE :
----------

Conceptually hum bol sakte hain:

    HttpSession → Redis

Lekin technically HttpSession object directly Redis me
store nahi hota.

Spring Session ek abstraction provide karta hai jo
Servlet HttpSession ko external session store (Redis) ke
saath integrate karta hai.

Simple language me:

    Application
         ↓
    HttpSession API
         ↓
    Spring Session
         ↓
       Redis
    (Session Data Store)


-------------------------------------------------------
## APPROACH 4 : STATELESS AUTHENTICATION (JWT)
-------------------------------------------------------

External Session Store (Redis) HttpSession ko
distributed environment me manage karne ki problem solve karta hai.

Ab application horizontally scale ki ja sakti hai aur
multiple application servers ek shared Redis Session Store
se same HttpSession access kar sakte hain.

Lekin ek important observation hai:

Redis use karne ke baad bhi application ko
user ki session state maintain karni pad rahi hai.


## QUESTION:
------------

Kya hum is server-side session state ko completely eliminate
kar sakte hain?


## ANSWER:
------------

Yes.

Is approach ko

        Stateless Authentication

kehte hain.


Stateless Authentication me server ko user ki authentication
aur authorization context ko HttpSession jaise server-side
session me maintain karna mandatory nahi hota.

Is purpose ke liye commonly

        JWT (JSON Web Token)

use kiya jata hai.


## IMPORTANT DISTINCTION: HTTP SESSION vs JWT
------------------------------------------------

## HTTP SESSION :
------------------

HttpSession server-side state maintain kar sakta hai, including:

    - Authentication Context
    - Authorization Context
    - Other Temporary User State

Yaani HttpSession me user ki identity, authentication status,
roles, permissions aur other temporary session-related
information maintain ki ja sakti hai.


HttpSession
    |
    ├── userId = 101
    ├── username = Anurag
    │
    │       → Identity
    │
    ├── authenticated = true
    │
    │       → Authentication Context
    │
    ├── roles = [USER]
    ├── permissions = [READ_ORDER, VIEW_PROFILE]
    │
    │       → Authorization Context
    │
    ├── language = "en"
    ├── theme = "dark"
    │
    │       → Other Temporary User/Session State
    │
    └── lastAccessTime = 10:30 AM
            │
            └── Session Metadata


## JWT:
---------

JWT HttpSession ka exact replacement nahi hai.

JWT khud Authentication ya Authorization mechanism nahi hai.

JWT ek token format hai jo Authentication aur
Authorization-related claims ko transmit karne ke
liye commonly use kiya jata hai.

Successful login ke baad server user ko ek JWT issue karta hai.

Har subsequent request ke saath client JWT bhejta hai,
aur application JWT ko verify karke usme available claims
ka use karke Authentication / Authorization perform kar sakti hai
before allowing access to the requested resource / API.

In Java/Spring Boot applications, frameworks such as
Spring Security JWT-based Authentication / Authorization
implement karne me help kar sakte hain.


## CAN JWT STORE ARBITRARY INFORMATION?
----------------------------------------

Technically JWT claims me arbitrary information rakhi ja sakti hai,
lekin generally unnecessary ya sensitive information JWT me
store nahi karni chahiye, kyunki JWT payload encrypted nahi hota
by default.

JWT ka payload Base64URL encoded hota hai, encrypted nahi.

Isliye payload ko decode karke uske contents easily read
kiye ja sakte hain.

Therefore:

        JWT ≠ Encrypted Data


Examples of information that should generally NOT be stored:

    - Passwords
    - Credit Card Numbers
    - OTPs
    - Highly Sensitive Personal Data
    - Secrets


## WHAT DOES A JWT ACTUALLY LOOK LIKE?
----------------------------------------

JWT generally ek long string hota hai:

eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9
.
eyJzdWIiOiIxMDEiLCJyb2xlIjoiVVNFUiIsImlhdCI6MTcyMDAwMDAwMCwiZXhwIjoxNzIwMDAzNjAwfQ
.
SflKxwRJSMeKKF2QT4fwpMeJf36POk6yJV_adQssw5c


Dhyan do:

JWT ke exactly 3 parts hote hain:

Header.Payload.Signature

Ye teen parts . character se separated hote hain.


## 1. HEADER
-------------

Header JWT ke baare me metadata contain karta hai.
Header generally batata hai ki token ko verify karne ke
liye kaunsa signing algorithm use kiya gaya hai.

Example:

{
  "alg": "HS256", // HS256 = HMAC + SHA-256
  "typ": "JWT"
}

Yahan:

alg → Algorithm used for signing
typ → Token type


# 2. PAYLOAD
----------------

Payload JWT ke andar actual information contain karta hai. Is information ko JWT claims kaha jata hai.

Example:

{
  "sub": "101",
  "name": "Anurag",
  "role": "USER",
  "iat": 1720000000,
  "exp": 1720003600
}


## REGISTERED CLAIMS vs CUSTOM CLAIMS
--------------------------------------

JWT claims broadly do categories me samjhe ja sakte hain:

    Registered Claims
            +
    Custom Claims


Registered Claims standardized claim names hain,
jaise:

    iss
    sub
    aud
    exp
    iat
    nbf
    jti


Custom Claims application ki requirement ke according
define kiye ja sakte hain.

Example:

{
    "sub": "101",
    "role": "USER",
    "department": "PAYMENTS"
    "permissions": [
        "READ_ORDER",
        "VIEW_PROFILE"
    ]
}


Yahan:

    sub
    → Registered Claim

    role
    → Custom Claim

    department
    → Custom Claim

    permissions
    → Custom Claim


1: AUTHENTICATION / IDENTITY RELATED CLAIMS
--------------------------------------------

`sub` ka matlab hai:

    Subject

JWT me 'sub' us entity ko identify karta hai jiske context me token issue kiya gaya hai.

Authentication ke context me, sub commonly user ke unique identifier ko represent karta hai.

Basically iska matlaba hai ye token kisko issue kiya gaya hai

Example:

    sub = "101"

ka matlab ho sakta hai:

    "Ye JWT User ID 101 ko represent karta hai."


Suppose jis user ko username aur password ke through authenticate kiya gaya hai, uski application Database me:

    User ID = 101

hai.

To server JWT issue karte waqt:

    sub = "101"

set kar sakta hai.

Iska matlab ho sakta hai:

    "Ye JWT User ID 101 ko represent karta hai."

Ab subsequent request me user/client jab ye JWT server ko bhejega, to server JWT ko verify karne ke baad identify kar sakta hai ki:

    "Request User 101 ke context me hai."



## IMPORTANT:
---------------

sub ka value necessarily Database ka numeric user_id hona mandatory nahi hai.

Application kisi bhi stable aur unique subject identifier ko use kar sakti hai.

Other identity-related claims application-specific ho sakte hain, for example:

    name
    userId
    username


2: AUTHORIZATION RELATED CLAIMS
--------------------------------

Authorization ka question hota hai:

    "Authenticated user kya kar sakta hai?"

## EXAMPLE:
------------

{
    "roles": ["USER"],
    "permissions": [
        "READ_ORDER",
        "VIEW_PROFILE"
    ]
}


Server JWT verify karne ke baad in claims ka use karke
decide kar sakta hai ki authenticated user ko
requested resource access karne ki permission hai ya nahi.

Example:

    role = USER
        ↓
    GET / order
        ↓
    Allowed


    role = USER
        ↓
    DELETE /admin/user/101
        ↓
    Not Allowed



## 3. TOKEN METADATA / VALIDITY CLAIMS
---------------------------------------

Ye claims primarily token ke metadata ko denote karte hai.


1. iss → Issuer
----------------

Token kis entity / service ne issue kiya hai.

Example:

    iss = "auth-service"

Meaning:

    → Ye token Auth Service ne issue kiya hai.

Server token receive karne ke baad verify kar sakta hai
ki token expected issuer ne hi issue kiya hai.

Spring Boot applications me Spring Security jaise frameworks
JWT validation ka kaam karte hai.


2. aud → Audience
------------------

Token kis intended service ke liye issue kiya gaya hai.

Example:

    aud = "order-service"

Meaning:

    → Ye token Order Service ke liye intended hai.

Agar same token ko Payment Service ko bheja jaye,
to Payment Service check kar sakti hai:

    aud == "payment-service" ?

Lekin token me:

    aud = "order-service"

hai.

To Payment Service token ko reject kar sakti hai,
kyunki token uske liye intended nahi hai.


3. exp → Expiration Time
-------------------------

Token kis time par expire hoga.

Example:

    "exp": 1720003600

1720003600 directly readable date/time nahi hai.
Ye ek Unix timestamp hai.

Maan lo iska meaning hai:

    Token expires at 10:00 AM

    10:00 AM
        ↓
    Token Expired
        ↓
    Server JWT ko reject karega


Iske baad client ko generally ek naya valid token
obtain karna padega.

Ye naya token:

    - Refresh Token mechanism ke through
      obtain kiya ja sakta hai.

    OR

    - Application ke authentication flow ke according
      user ko dobara login karna pad sakta hai.


4. iat → Issued At
-------------------

Ye batata hai ki JWT token kab issue / generate hua tha.

Example:

    "iat": 1719990000

1719990000 bhi ek Unix timestamp hai.

Maan lo:

    iat = 9:00 AM
    exp = 10:00 AM

To token:

    9:00 AM
       ↓
    Issued
       ↓
    10:00 AM
       ↓
    Expired

Yaani token ka validity period approximately
9:00 AM se 10:00 AM tak hai.


5. nbf → Not Before
--------------------

Ye specify karta hai ki token kis time se valid hona
start hoga.

Example:

    "nbf": 1719993600

1719993600 ek Unix timestamp hai.

Maan lo:

    Token generated → 9:00 AM
    nbf            → 9:05 AM
    exp            → 10:00 AM


    9:00 AM ─────── 9:05 AM ───────── 10:00 AM
       ❌                ✅                  ❌
    Not Valid          Valid             Expired


Yaani token 9:00 AM par generate ho gaya,
lekin 9:05 AM se pehle use nahi kiya ja sakta.


6. jti → JWT ID
----------------

JWT ka unique identifier.

Example:

    jti = "ABC123"


Suppose user login karta hai aur usse ek JWT issue hota hai:

    JWT-1
    jti = ABC123


Ab token expire hone se pehle user dobara login karta hai
aur server ek naya JWT issue karta hai:

    JWT-2
    jti = XYZ789


Dono tokens:

    JWT-1 → jti = ABC123
    JWT-2 → jti = XYZ789

same user ko represent kar sakte hain,
lekin dono JWTs ki unique identity alag hai.

Agar dono tokens ki validity abhi khatam nahi hui hai,
to dono tokens simultaneously valid ho sakte hain.


## IMPORTANT:
-------------

JWT stateless hone ki wajah se server automatically
purane JWT ko sirf isliye invalidate nahi karta
kyunki user ne login karke ek naya JWT obtain kar liya hai.

Example:

    Old Token
    jti = ABC123
    exp = 10:00 AM

User logs in again:

    New Token
    jti = XYZ789
    exp = 11:00 AM


Agar Old Token abhi bhi expire nahi hua hai,
to wo normally valid reh sakta hai.

Agar requirement ho:

    "User ke naye login ke baad
     purana token immediately invalid ho jana chahiye."

to pure stateless JWT approach se ye automatically
achieve nahi hota.

Server ko additional state maintain karni padegi,
for example:

    - Revoked JWT IDs (JTI blacklist)
    - Token Version
    - Session / Token Version Number

Is additional state ko use karke server old tokens ko
explicitly reject kar sakta hai.


## 3. SIGNATURE
-----------------

JWT ka Payload Base64URL encoded hota hai, encrypted nahi.

Isliye Payload ko decode karke uske contents
easily read kiye ja sakte hain aur modify bhi kiye ja sakte hain.


Agar JWT me Signature nahi hoti,
to Application ke paas ye reliably verify karne ka
mechanism nahi hota ki:

    "Kya ye Header/Payload trusted / intended issuer
     ne actually generate kiya tha?"


Isi problem ko solve karne ke liye JWT me
Signature use ki jaati hai.


Signature Application ko ye verify karne me help karti hai ki
JWT issue hone ke baad uske Header ya Payload ko
tamper / modify nahi kiya gaya hai.

Application generally Security Framework
(e.g. Spring Security) ki help se JWT ki Signature verify karti hai.



### HOW IS THE SIGNATURE GENERATED?
------------------------------------

JWT issue karte waqt trusted Application / Service
Header + Payload ko Signing Key ke saath
sign karti hai aur ek Signature generate karti hai.


HS256 ke case me Signature conceptually:

    HMAC-SHA256(
        Base64URL(Header)
        + "."
        + Base64URL(Payload),
        Secret Key
    )

Yahan:

    Signing Input =
        Base64URL(Header)
        + "."
        + Base64URL(Payload)


### SYMMETRIC SIGNING — HS256
------------------------------

Symmetric algorithm (e.g. HS256) ke case me:

    Signing Key = Verification Key

Yaani:

    Issuer and Target Application / Service dono ke paas same Secret Key hoti hai.

Issuer Service
      |
      | Same Secret Key
      ↓
    SIGN JWT


Target Service
      |
      | Same Secret Key
      ↓
   VERIFY JWT



### ASYMMETRIC SIGNING — RS256
-------------------------------

Asymmetric algorithm (e.g. RS256) ke case me:

    Signing Key      → Private Key
    Verification Key → Public Key


Issuer Application / Service
        |
        | Private Key
        ↓
      SIGN JWT


Target Application / Service
        |
        | Public Key
        ↓
     VERIFY JWT


Yaani Issuer Private Key se JWT sign karta hai,
aur Target Application / Service corresponding
Public Key se Signature verify karti hai.


### HOW DOES SIGNATURE VERIFICATION WORK?
------------------------------------------

Baad me jab Target Application / Service JWT receive karti hai,
to generally Security Framework (e.g. Spring Security)
ki help se JWT ki Signature verify ki jaati hai.

Verification ke liye Target Application / Service
JWT ke Header + Payload se expected Signature
calculate karti hai using the appropriate verification key.

Phir:

    Expected Signature
            vs
    Received Signature
            ↓
        Compare


Agar dono Signatures match karti hain:

    Signature Valid
          ↓
    Header + Payload
    were not modified
          ↓
    JWT passes Signature Verification


Agar Signatures match nahi karti:

    Signature Invalid
          ↓
    JWT may have been modified
    or Signature is otherwise invalid
          ↓
    Request Rejected
       

## COMPLETE JWT AUTHENTICATION FLOW — SPRING BOOT
---------------------------------------------------

Suppose humare paas ek Spring Boot application hai
jo JWT-based authentication use karti hai.


## 1. DEVELOPER NE LOGIN ENDPOINT BANAYA
-----------------------------------------

Example:

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/auth")
public class LoginController {

    @PostMapping("/login")
    public LoginResponse login(@RequestBody LoginRequest request) {
        // authenticate user
        // generate JWT
        // return JWT
    }
}


Endpoint:

    POST /api/auth/login



## 2. LOGIN REQUEST SPRING BOOT APPLICATION TAK PAHUNCHTI HAI
---------------------------------------------------------------

User login page par credentials enter karta hai:

    username = anurag
    password = ******


Client request bhejta hai:

    POST /api/auth/login

{
    "username": "anurag",
    "password": "password123"
}


Request Controller tak pahunchne se pehle
Spring Security Filter Chain se pass hoti hai.

Login endpoint ko normally `permitAll()` kiya jata hai,
taaki user ko login karne ke liye pehle se authenticated
hona na pade.


Example:

@Bean
SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {

    http.authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
        .requestMatchers("/api/auth/login").permitAll()
        .anyRequest().authenticated()
    );

    return http.build();
}


Ab request LoginController ke `/login` method tak pahunchti hai.

Controller khud username/password ko Database se compare nahi karta.

Authentication Spring Security ke authentication components
ke through perform hoti hai.



## 3. AUTHENTICATION PROCESS
----------------------------

Controller credentials ko `AuthenticationManager` ko deta hai:

Authentication authentication =
    authenticationManager.authenticate(
        new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(
            request.getUsername(),
            request.getPassword()
        )
    );


### 3.1 AUTHENTICATIONMANAGER
----------------------------

`AuthenticationManager` generally authentication khud perform nahi karta.

Ye authentication process ko appropriate
`AuthenticationProvider` ko delegate karta hai.

Username/password based authentication ke liye commonly:

    DaoAuthenticationProvider

use kiya jata hai.

Different authentication mechanisms ke liye
different AuthenticationProviders ho sakte hain.

For example:

    Username/Password → DaoAuthenticationProvider
    LDAP              → LdapAuthenticationProvider
    OAuth2/OIDC       → OAuth2/OIDC-specific providers


Flow:

    LoginController
          ↓
    AuthenticationManager
          ↓
    AuthenticationProvider



### 3.2 USERDETAILSSERVICE AND UserDetails
------------------------------------------

`AuthenticationProvider` ko user ki details chahiye hoti hain.

Username/password based authentication me
`DaoAuthenticationProvider` commonly `UserDetailsService`
ka use karta hai.

`UserDetailsService` ka kaam hai:

    Username
       ↓
    User details load karo


Example:

    username = "anurag"


UserDetailsService Database se user ko find karta hai:

    Database
       ↓
    userId = 101
    username = anurag
    password = <hashed-password>
    roles = USER


Database se retrieved information ko Spring Security
`UserDetails` object ke form me represent karti hai.

Conceptually:

    UserDetails
       |
       ├── username
       ├── password (stored hash)
       └── authorities / roles


Flow:

    LoginController
          ↓
    AuthenticationManager
          ↓
    AuthenticationProvider
          ↓
    UserDetailsService
          ↓
    Database
          ↓
    UserDetails



### 3.3 PASSWORDENCODER
-----------------------

Ab Spring Security ke paas:

    Provided Password
            +
    Stored Password Hash

available hain.


#### PASSWORD STORAGE
---------------------

User ka password Database me plain-text form me
store nahi kiya jata.

Password ko store karne se pehle application
`PasswordEncoder.encode()` ka use karke
password ka hash generate karti hai.

Example:

    "password123"
          ↓
    PasswordEncoder.encode()
          ↓
    BCrypt Hash
          ↓
    Database


#### PASSWORD VERIFICATION
--------------------------

Login ke time:

    User enters:
        "password123"

             ↓

    PasswordEncoder.matches(
        "password123",
        $2a$10$....hashed-password
    )

             ↓

    Stored hash ko inspect karta hai
             ↓
    Usme se hashing algorithm + parameters + salt extract karta hai
             ↓
    Provided password ko SAME algorithm, SAME parameters aur SAME salt
    ke saath process karta hai
             ↓
    Result ko DB stored password hash se compare karta hai


If the password is valid:

    Authentication Successful
            ↓
    AuthenticationProvider
            ↓
    Returns authenticated Authentication Object
            ↓
    AuthenticationManager
            ↓
    Returns Authentication Object to Controller


Agar password invalid hai:

    Authentication Failed
            ↓
    AuthenticationException
            ↓
    Login Failed
            ↓
    Usually 401 Unauthorized


## NOTE:
---------

PasswordEncoder
      ↓
does NOT decrypt the database hash

Because password hash generally one-way hota hai.



### 3.4 AUTHENTICATION OBJECT
----------------------------

`Authentication` object Spring Security me
authenticated user ko represent karta hai.

Authentication successful hone ke baad conceptually
isme information ho sakti hai:

    Authentication
       |
       ├── Principal
       |      └── UserDetails
       |             ├── username = anurag
       |             ├── userId = 101
       |             └── ...
       |
       ├── Authorities
       |      └── ROLE_USER
       |
       └── Authenticated
              └── true


Yahi authenticated `Authentication` object
Controller ko return hota hai.



## 4. JWT GENERATION
--------------------

Authentication successful hone ke baad
Controller ke paas authenticated user ki information
`Authentication` object ke through available hoti hai.

Application isi information ke basis par
JWT claims aur token prepare karti hai.



## 5. JWT RETURNED TO CLIENT
-----------------------------

JWT generate aur sign hone ke baad
Login Controller JWT ko response me client ko return karta hai.

Example:

{
    "accessToken": "<JWT>"
}


Ab client ke paas valid JWT hai.

Client JWT ko securely store karta hai, commonly an HttpOnly + Secure cookie ke andar (web applications me).

Client subsequent authenticated requests me
isi JWT ko send karega.


## 6. WHAT HAPPENS DURING SUBSEQUENT REQUESTS?
-------------------------------------------------

Ab user ke paas valid JWT hai.

Suppose user orders dekhna chahta hai:

    GET /api/orders

User ka client JWT ke saath request bhejta hai.

Common HTTP format:

    Authorization: Bearer <JWT>


Example:

    GET /api/orders
    Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIs...


Ab request Spring Boot application tak pahunchti hai.


## 6.1 REQUEST ENTERS SPRING SECURITY FILTER CHAIN
----------------------------------------------------

Request directly Controller tak nahi jaati.

Sabse pehle request Spring Security Filter Chain
ke `BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter` se pass hoti hai.

`BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter` HTTP/HTTPS request ke
`Authorization` header ko check karta hai.

Expected format:

    Authorization: Bearer <JWT>


`BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter` `Authorization` header
read karta hai aur `Bearer ` prefix remove karta hai.

Uske baad Spring Security internally
`BearerTokenAuthenticationToken` create karta hai,
jisme extracted Bearer Token, i.e. actual JWT, hota hai.


Agar `Authorization` header hi missing hai,
to protected endpoint ke liye authentication establish
nahi ho payegi.

Result generally:

    401 Unauthorized


## NOTE:
---------

`BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter` Spring Security ka
built-in filter hai.

`JwtAuthenticationFilter` ek custom filter ka naam hai
jo developers khud create kar sakte hain.

Example:

    public class JwtAuthenticationFilter
            extends OncePerRequestFilter {
        ...
    }


Modern Spring Security Resource Server setup me
hume necessarily apna custom `JwtAuthenticationFilter`
banane ki zarurat nahi hoti.

Built-in `BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter`
JWT authentication flow initiate karta hai.


## 6.2 AUTHENTICATIONMANAGER
------------------------------

Ab `BearerTokenAuthenticationToken`
`AuthenticationManager` ko pass hota hai.

`AuthenticationManager` authentication ko
appropriate `AuthenticationProvider` ko delegate karta hai.

JWT Resource Server configuration me commonly:

    JwtAuthenticationProvider

use hota hai.


## 6.3 JWT AUTHENTICATION PROVIDER
-----------------------------------

`JwtAuthenticationProvider` ka kaam JWT ko authenticate karna hai.

Ye `JwtDecoder` ka use karta hai.


## 6.4 JWT DECODER
------------------

`JwtDecoder` JWT ko decode karta hai aur
JWT ki cryptographic verification/validation
perform karta hai.


Conceptually:


1. SIGNATURE VALIDATION
------------------------

For HS256:

    Secret Key
        ↓
    Signature Verification


For RS256:

    Public Key
        ↓
    Signature Verification


2. CLAIMS VALIDATION
---------------------

Configured validation rules ke according
JWT ke claims validate kiye ja sakte hain:

    exp
    iss
    aud
    nbf
    ...


## 6.5 JWT → JwtAuthenticationConverter → Authentication
----------------------------------------------------------

JWT successfully validate hone ke baad
Spring Security ko JWT ke andar available
user identity aur authorities ko
Authentication representation me convert karna hota hai.


Example JWT:

{
    "sub": "101",
    "roles": ["USER"]
}


`JwtAuthenticationConverter` JWT ke claims ko
Spring Security ke Authentication representation
me convert karne me help karta hai.


Conceptually:

    JWT
     |
     ├── sub = 101
     └── roles = USER
             |
             ↓
    JwtAuthenticationConverter
             |
             ↓
    JwtAuthenticationToken
             |
             ├── Principal = User 101
             └── Authorities = USER

## NOTE:
---------
JwtAuthenticationToken Spring Security ka Authentication implementation hai.
Authentication ek interface hai jo authenticated user's information ko represent karne ka contract provide karta hai.
JwtAuthenticationToken us interface ki concrete implementation hai.


## 6.6 AUTHENTICATION SECURITY CONTEXT ME SET HOTA HAI
-------------------------------------------------------

Successful authentication ke baad `Authentication` object ko current request ke  `SecurityContext` me set kiya jata hai.
JwtAuthenticationToken ko Authentication ke roop me SecurityContext me set kiya jata hai.

Ab current request ke processing ke dauran
Spring Security ke paas authenticated user ko
represent karne ke liye `Authentication` object available hai.


Conceptually:

    SecurityContext
         |
         └── JwtAuthenticationToken
                 |
                 ├── User = 101
                 └── Authorities = USER


## 6.7 AUTHORIZATION
--------------------

JWT validate hone ke baad authenticated
`Authentication` object `SecurityContext` me
available hota hai.

Ab Spring Security check karti hai:

    "Kya is user ko requested resource
     access karne ki permission hai?"


Authorization ke liye `Authentication` object me
available Authorities / Roles use kiye jaate hain.


Example:

    Authentication
        |
        ├── User = 101
        └── Authority = ROLE_USER


Request:

    GET /api/orders


Required Authority:

    ROLE_USER


Available Authority in Authentication object:

    ROLE_USER


        ↓

    Authorization PASS
        ↓
    Request Continue


Agar API ko access karne ke liye required Authority
user ke paas available nahi hai:

    Authorization FAIL
        ↓
    403 Forbidden


Authentication:

    "Who are you?"


Authorization:

    "What are you allowed to do?"


## 6.8 REQUEST CONTROLLER / ENDPOINT TAK PAHUNCHTI HAI
------------------------------------------------------

After successful Authentication and Authorization,
Spring Security Filter Chain request ko continue karti hai
aur request application ke relevant Controller endpoint
tak pahunchti hai.


Example:

    @GetMapping("/api/orders")
    public List<Order> getOrders(Authentication authentication) {

        return orderService.getOrders();

    }


Ab Controller ko current authenticated user ki
information Spring Security ke `SecurityContext`
ke through available ho sakti hai.


Controller request ko business processing ke liye
relevant Service layer ko delegate karta hai.

Example:

    Controller
        ↓
    Service
        ↓
    Repository
        ↓
    Database


Service business logic process karta hai aur
required result Controller ko return karta hai.

Controller us result ko HTTP Response ke form me
client/user ko return kar deta hai.
